2. Mutable vs Immutable
Immutable: Cannot be changed after creation
int,float, bool, str, tuple, frozenset

#Example:  x = 10
Memory:   x -----> 10
          x = x + 5
Does Python change 10? -> No
Instead it creates a new object
Old: x -----> 10
New: 10
x -----> 15
The object 10 never changes -> A new object 15 is created

Mutable: Can be modified
list,dict, set, bytearray

3. Assignment vs Copy
Assignment
a = [10,20]
b = a
b.append(30)
print(a) #[10,20,30]
#Why Because both variables refer to the same object

##Copy
a = [10,20]
b = a.copy() #Now there are two different list objects
b.append(30)
a = [10,20]
b = [10,20,30]

##4. Shallow Copy vs Deep Copy
data = [
    [1,2],
    [3,4]
    ]
#Shallow Copy
import copy
new = copy.copy(data)
#The outer list is copied -> The inner lists are shared
new[0].append(99) #data [[1,2,99],
                         [3,4]]

╰┈➤𝗦𝗰𝗵𝗼𝗼𝗹 𝗘𝘅𝗮𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲
The classroom contains
Bench A
Bench B

#Shallow Copy - You create another classroom
But instead of buying new benches, you simply place the same benches in the second classroom
There are two classrooms, But only one Bench A
If someone paints Bench A red, both classrooms now show a red Bench A

#Deep Copy
You create another classroom
You buy
New Bench A
New Bench B  --> Painting one bench affects only that classroom

╰┈➤Shallow Copy: Creates a new outer container, but the elements inside are still references to the original nested objects. Nested mutable objects are shared
╰┈➤Deep Copy: Creates a completely independent copy by recursively copying every nested object. No mutable objects are shared
                                    Shallow Copy = Copy the box,share the items inside ardmindhaa
                                    Deep Copy = Copy the box and copy every item inside the box

Concept                            Key Idea                                                                             
 Variable             | A name that refers to an object                                                      
 Object               | Data stored in memory                                                                
 Mutable              | Object can be modified in place (e.g., list, dict, set)                        
 Immutable            | Object cannot be modified; changes create a new object (e.g., int, str, tuple) 
 Assignment (`b = a`) | Creates another reference to the same object                                         
 Copy (`a.copy()`)    | Creates a new outer object (for lists)                                               
 Shallow Copy         | Copies only the outer container; nested objects are still shared                     
 Deep Copy            | Recursively copies all nested objects                                                
 Reference            | The link from a variable name to an object in memory                                 

##A simple rule to remember
-> Assignment creates another reference
-> Copy creates another object
-> Mutable objects can change in place
-> Immutable objects never change; operations create new objects instead

